# The system calls, described once.
#
# Everything that has to agree about a call is generated from this list by
# //build/abi/gen_syscalls.py: the private call numbers the kernel and the vDSO
# share, the vDSO stubs, the extern "C" declarations <ours/vdso.hpp> includes,
# the kernel's typed syscall::<name> declarations (no sys_ prefix) and the shims
# that unpack a register frame into them. GN derives libours.ifs, which the
# vDSO's exports are checked against, from the same list. Before this file each
# of those was written by hand, in four places, kept in step by nothing but
# care; a handler whose definition drifts from the entry here now fails to link
# (design 4.7).
#
# Adding a call is two things by hand: an entry here, and the body of
# syscall::<name>() in //kernel/lib/syscall.
#
# The position of an entry is its call number. The numbers are private to the
# kernel and the vDSO -- a program names a call by its vDSO symbol, never by a
# number -- so reordering is allowed, but it changes both sides at once and
# nothing else, which is exactly what a private number is for.
#
# Each entry:
#
#   name    The call, without the ours_ prefix. The vDSO exports ours_<name>,
#           a program calls ours::<name> (<ours/vdso.hpp>), and the kernel
#           defines syscall::<name>.
#   brief   One line.
#   doc     More lines, as a list of strings. Written for the program that
#           calls it; what the kernel does inside belongs with syscall::<name>.
#   args    In order, at most 8.
#           Each is a scope:
#             name     The parameter's name.
#             type     A primitive (u8 .. u64, i8 .. i64, usize, isize, char,
#                      void) or a type in namespace ours.
#             dir      Only on a pointer, and what makes it one: "in" (the
#                      kernel reads through it) or "out" (it writes).
#             count    Only on a pointer: the name of the argument that holds
#                      its length in elements. Without one the pointer names
#                      exactly one element.
#             release  Only on a Uandle passed by value or as an input
#                      array: true when the call consumes the handle whatever
#                      its outcome. An array is consumed once the kernel has
#                      read it: a count past the call's limit, or an array it
#                      cannot read, consumes nothing.
#   attrs   Any of "blocking" (may wait; the stub makes the call again when it
#           answers InternalIrqRetry, which a suspend ending the wait does, so a
#           blocking call must be one that can be made again from the start:
#           its interrupted wait changed nothing, or only what doing it again
#           repeats harmlessly; a call that changes something and then waits,
#           like a send followed by a wait for the reply, is not, and has to be
#           split into a call and a finish), "noreturn" (never comes back; the
#           stub ends in ud2), "debug" (a debug facility with no counterpart
#           in a finished system). A blocking call returns Status.
#   ret     The return type, "Status" when left out: "Status" unless the call
#           returns a plain value, and "void" exactly when it is noreturn.

